1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,240
Outbreak questions hand you a table or a

2
00:00:02,320 --> 00:00:04,720
graph and ask what's going on.

3
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:07,200
Most of them turn on one habit.

4
00:00:07,360 --> 00:00:10,560
Before you compare anything, find the denominator, the

5
00:00:10,640 --> 00:00:12,240
number you divide by.

6
00:00:12,480 --> 00:00:14,560
Then link the numbers to a cause, and

7
00:00:14,640 --> 00:00:16,720
the cause to a control.

8
00:00:16,800 --> 00:00:19,600
Here are three HSC-style questions.

9
00:00:19,840 --> 00:00:22,080
Pause and try each one, then we'll work

10
00:00:22,160 --> 00:00:23,040
through it together.

11
00:00:24,267 --> 00:00:25,066
Question one.

12
00:00:25,147 --> 00:00:28,666
Sixty students arrive at Camp Wollemi on Monday.

13
00:00:29,307 --> 00:00:32,586
Everyone eats chicken pasta for dinner, and overnight,

14
00:00:32,667 --> 00:00:33,946
eighteen fall ill.

15
00:00:34,507 --> 00:00:37,306
On Tuesday, the well students have lunch.

16
00:00:37,627 --> 00:00:40,666
Some eat sandwiches made from Monday's leftover chicken,

17
00:00:40,707 --> 00:00:42,506
and the rest eat salad rolls.

18
00:00:43,067 --> 00:00:45,546
That night, sixteen more fall ill.

19
00:00:46,187 --> 00:00:48,586
Calculate the percentage who fell ill in each

20
00:00:48,667 --> 00:00:49,306
lunch group.

21
00:00:49,707 --> 00:00:52,506
Then evaluate the director's claim that the illness

22
00:00:52,667 --> 00:00:54,186
spread from person to person.

23
00:00:54,827 --> 00:00:56,106
Pause here to answer.

24
00:01:06,534 --> 00:01:08,053
Start with the denominator.

25
00:01:08,534 --> 00:01:11,333
The question asks about each lunch group, so

26
00:01:11,414 --> 00:01:14,053
divide by the number who ate each lunch,

27
00:01:14,134 --> 00:01:15,493
not by the whole camp.

28
00:01:16,054 --> 00:01:18,373
Sixteen of the twenty-eight who ate the sandwiches

29
00:01:18,454 --> 00:01:19,093
fell ill.

30
00:01:19,494 --> 00:01:21,573
That's about fifty-seven per cent.

31
00:01:21,894 --> 00:01:23,813
None of the fourteen who ate salad rolls

32
00:01:23,894 --> 00:01:24,453
fell ill.

33
00:01:24,774 --> 00:01:25,573
That's zero.

34
00:01:26,134 --> 00:01:28,693
Now the two groups compare fairly, because each

35
00:01:28,774 --> 00:01:30,613
number is out of the people who had

36
00:01:30,694 --> 00:01:31,493
that food.

37
00:01:32,700 --> 00:01:33,940
Now the claim.

38
00:01:34,220 --> 00:01:35,820
Read the epidemic curve.

39
00:01:36,060 --> 00:01:38,780
There are two sharp clusters, each the night

40
00:01:38,860 --> 00:01:40,940
after a meal with the chicken, about eight

41
00:01:41,020 --> 00:01:44,460
to eighteen hours later, and nothing in between.

42
00:01:44,580 --> 00:01:46,620
That fits the six to twenty-four hours this

43
00:01:46,660 --> 00:01:48,300
bacterium takes.

44
00:01:48,340 --> 00:01:50,860
If it spread from person to person, cases

45
00:01:50,980 --> 00:01:53,500
would usually keep appearing in rising waves for

46
00:01:54,020 --> 00:01:54,593
days.

47
00:01:54,647 --> 00:01:56,620
And the salad roll group shared cabins with

48
00:01:56,660 --> 00:01:59,260
sick students, yet none fell ill.

49
00:01:59,300 --> 00:02:01,180
So the claim isn't supported.

50
00:02:01,260 --> 00:02:04,300
It was one food source, eaten twice.

51
00:02:04,340 --> 00:02:06,700
So the control that fits is cooling cooked

52
00:02:06,780 --> 00:02:09,980
meat quickly and throwing out leftovers, because this

53
00:02:10,020 --> 00:02:13,100
bacterium multiplies in meat that cools slowly.

54
00:02:14,300 --> 00:02:17,580
The classic mistake is dividing by sixty.

55
00:02:17,700 --> 00:02:20,220
Sixteen out of sixty is about twenty-seven per

56
00:02:20,380 --> 00:02:22,860
cent, but that mixes in students who never

57
00:02:22,980 --> 00:02:25,420
ate the sandwiches, so it can't show which

58
00:02:25,540 --> 00:02:26,740
food was the problem.

59
00:02:27,020 --> 00:02:29,260
Always divide by the people who could have

60
00:02:29,340 --> 00:02:29,900
caught it.

61
00:02:31,100 --> 00:02:31,980
Question two.

62
00:02:32,460 --> 00:02:35,100
A province in South East Asia had malaria.

63
00:02:35,580 --> 00:02:38,700
From twenty nineteen, every household got treated bed

64
00:02:38,900 --> 00:02:41,580
nets, and village health workers began testing and

65
00:02:41,660 --> 00:02:43,980
treating fevers on the same day.

66
00:02:44,300 --> 00:02:47,180
The table gives the population, the new cases,

67
00:02:47,220 --> 00:02:49,740
and a blood survey, for twenty eighteen and

68
00:02:49,860 --> 00:02:51,020
twenty twenty-three.

69
00:02:51,340 --> 00:02:54,540
Calculate the incidence per thousand people per year,

70
00:02:54,620 --> 00:02:56,940
and the prevalence of infection, for each year.

71
00:02:57,420 --> 00:03:00,220
Then explain how each measure could cause these

72
00:03:00,300 --> 00:03:01,340
changes.

73
00:03:01,500 --> 00:03:02,700
Pause here to answer.

74
00:03:13,100 --> 00:03:14,460
Incidence first.

75
00:03:14,700 --> 00:03:17,180
That's new cases in a year, divided by

76
00:03:17,260 --> 00:03:20,220
the people at risk, here the whole population.

77
00:03:20,860 --> 00:03:24,140
For twenty eighteen, four thousand eight hundred, divided

78
00:03:24,220 --> 00:03:27,180
by four hundred thousand, times a thousand, is

79
00:03:27,260 --> 00:03:28,540
twelve per thousand.

80
00:03:29,100 --> 00:03:32,300
For twenty twenty-three, eight hundred and forty, divided

81
00:03:32,380 --> 00:03:34,780
by four hundred and twenty thousand, times a

82
00:03:34,820 --> 00:03:36,580
thousand, is two.

83
00:03:37,020 --> 00:03:40,140
Prevalence is the share infected at one time,

84
00:03:40,220 --> 00:03:43,100
so its denominator is the people tested.

85
00:03:43,180 --> 00:03:45,180
Fifty of two thousand is two and a

86
00:03:45,300 --> 00:03:46,460
half per cent.

87
00:03:46,620 --> 00:03:48,780
Six of two thousand is zero point three

88
00:03:48,940 --> 00:03:49,580
per cent.

89
00:03:50,800 --> 00:03:52,960
Now link each measure to the tap or

90
00:03:53,040 --> 00:03:53,880
the tank.

91
00:03:54,160 --> 00:03:56,960
Bed nets mean fewer bites by Anopheles, which

92
00:03:57,040 --> 00:03:59,840
bite mostly at night, so fewer people get

93
00:03:59,920 --> 00:04:00,880
new infections.

94
00:04:01,440 --> 00:04:04,480
That turns down the tap, so incidence falls,

95
00:04:04,520 --> 00:04:06,640
and with less flowing in, the tank falls

96
00:04:06,720 --> 00:04:07,120
too.

97
00:04:07,760 --> 00:04:11,200
Same-day testing and treatment clears each infection sooner,

98
00:04:11,280 --> 00:04:13,600
so people stay infected for less time.

99
00:04:14,080 --> 00:04:16,880
That drains the tank faster, so prevalence falls

100
00:04:16,960 --> 00:04:17,440
further.

101
00:04:17,920 --> 00:04:20,720
And with fewer infected people, mosquitoes pick up

102
00:04:20,800 --> 00:04:24,240
fewer parasites, so new cases fall even more.

103
00:04:25,467 --> 00:04:28,346
The classic mistake is dividing the fifty positives

104
00:04:28,427 --> 00:04:29,706
by the whole province.

105
00:04:30,187 --> 00:04:33,226
Only two thousand people were tested, so fifty

106
00:04:33,387 --> 00:04:36,186
out of four hundred thousand makes infection look

107
00:04:36,267 --> 00:04:38,666
two hundred times rarer than it is.

108
00:04:39,867 --> 00:04:41,066
Question three.

109
00:04:41,387 --> 00:04:43,866
After a flood in Town V, the water

110
00:04:43,947 --> 00:04:46,986
supply and toilets were damaged, and cholera broke

111
00:04:47,067 --> 00:04:47,946
out.

112
00:04:47,987 --> 00:04:50,746
The graph shows new cases each week.

113
00:04:50,827 --> 00:04:53,786
In week three, health workers quarantined the household

114
00:04:53,867 --> 00:04:56,426
of every case for five days.

115
00:04:56,467 --> 00:04:59,706
In week six, they chlorinated all drinking water

116
00:04:59,747 --> 00:05:01,546
and repaired the toilets.

117
00:05:01,627 --> 00:05:04,666
Using the graph, identify which measure was followed

118
00:05:04,747 --> 00:05:06,746
by a fall in cases.

119
00:05:06,827 --> 00:05:11,386
Then justify using environmental management, rather than quarantine,

120
00:05:11,427 --> 00:05:13,226
to control this outbreak.

121
00:05:13,387 --> 00:05:14,586
Pause here to answer.

122
00:05:25,000 --> 00:05:27,280
Read the graph against the two arrows.

123
00:05:27,560 --> 00:05:30,520
After the quarantine in week three, cases kept

124
00:05:30,600 --> 00:05:33,680
rising, from fifteen to thirty-eight.

125
00:05:34,040 --> 00:05:37,320
After chlorination in week six, cases fell within

126
00:05:37,360 --> 00:05:40,840
a week, from thirty-eight to twenty-two, then to

127
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:42,640
one by week ten.

128
00:05:43,080 --> 00:05:45,640
Cases didn't stop at once, because people who

129
00:05:45,720 --> 00:05:49,160
swallowed the bacteria just before chlorination could still

130
00:05:49,240 --> 00:05:51,560
fall ill up to five days later.

131
00:05:52,767 --> 00:05:54,046
Now justify.

132
00:05:54,527 --> 00:05:57,966
Cholera spreads mainly through water or food contaminated

133
00:05:58,047 --> 00:05:58,846
with faeces.

134
00:05:59,407 --> 00:06:02,126
Chlorine kills the bacteria in the water, and

135
00:06:02,207 --> 00:06:04,286
repaired toilets keep faeces out of it.

136
00:06:04,847 --> 00:06:07,486
So environmental management breaks the travel link for

137
00:06:07,567 --> 00:06:09,886
everyone at once, and nobody has to be

138
00:06:09,967 --> 00:06:10,766
sick first.

139
00:06:11,407 --> 00:06:14,606
Quarantine only holds the households of known cases.

140
00:06:15,087 --> 00:06:17,486
But most infected people have mild or no

141
00:06:17,567 --> 00:06:20,686
symptoms, and still pass the bacteria, so many

142
00:06:20,767 --> 00:06:23,486
are never found, and the water keeps infecting

143
00:06:23,567 --> 00:06:24,846
new people anyway.

144
00:06:26,067 --> 00:06:29,106
The classic mistake is writing that quarantine works

145
00:06:29,227 --> 00:06:31,946
because it keeps infected people away from others.

146
00:06:32,227 --> 00:06:34,306
That helps when people pass a disease to

147
00:06:34,387 --> 00:06:34,866
each other.

148
00:06:35,347 --> 00:06:37,666
Here, it was mainly the water passing it

149
00:06:37,787 --> 00:06:38,146
on.

150
00:06:39,367 --> 00:06:41,446
So here's how to start each type.

151
00:06:41,927 --> 00:06:44,886
For a table, find the denominator: who could

152
00:06:44,967 --> 00:06:47,206
have caught it, or who was tested.

153
00:06:47,687 --> 00:06:50,406
For a change in incidence or prevalence, ask

154
00:06:50,527 --> 00:06:52,926
whether the tap or the tank changed.

155
00:06:53,127 --> 00:06:55,686
For a control, find the route, then the

156
00:06:55,767 --> 00:06:58,446
link the measure breaks, and check the timing.

157
00:06:58,727 --> 00:07:01,926
Next, how disease control ideas have changed over

158
00:07:02,007 --> 00:07:02,566
time.

159
00:07:03,767 --> 00:07:05,206
Here are the key notes.

160
00:07:05,447 --> 00:07:07,366
Pause the video if you need more time

161
00:07:07,447 --> 00:07:08,246
to copy them.
